# Form fields

The basic field set: `input`, `label`, `textarea`, `select` and
`switch` — native form elements styled by basecoat. There is no partial
for these: write the native element with its basecoat class inline.

## Input

A native `<input>` with the `input` class — basecoat styles it
directly, so write it inline.

<%= embed :form_fields, :input %>

```html
<input class="input" type="email" placeholder="name@nhs.net">
```

## Textarea

A native `<textarea>` with the `textarea` class — write it inline.

<%= embed :form_fields, :textarea %>

```html
<textarea class="textarea" placeholder="Clinical notes" rows="4"></textarea>
```

## Label

A native `<label>` with the `label` class, wired to its control with
`for`. Clicking the label focuses the field.

<%= embed :form_fields, :label %>

```html
<label class="label" for="email">Email</label>
<input class="input" id="email" type="email" placeholder="name@nhs.net">
```

## Select

A native `<select>` with the `select` class — write it inline.

<%= embed :form_fields, :select %>

```html
<select class="select">
  <option>Leeds</option>
  <option>Bradford</option>
</select>
```

## Switch

A native checkbox with `role="switch"` and the `input` class — basecoat
styles it as a toggle. Wrap it in a `label` to give it a hit target.

<%= embed :form_fields, :switch %>

```html
<label class="label">
  <input type="checkbox" role="switch" class="input">
  Email notifications
</label>
```

## When to use

- `input` — short free text (and `type:` variants: date, number,
  email).
- `textarea` — long free text: notes, descriptions.
- `select` — one of many known options, space-tight.
- `switch` — on/off applied immediately, outside a submit flow.
- `label` — every visible field, wired by `for:`.

## When not to use

- Two or three options in a `select` — use `radio_group`; all
  options visible beats hidden behind a click.
- A long or unfamiliar option list in a `select` — use `combobox`
  (searchable).
- A `switch` inside a form that has a submit button — use `checkbox`
  (a switch implies the change already happened). See the *Forms*
  pattern page.

## Anatomy and options

Each is a native element carrying its basecoat class — `input`,
`textarea`, `label`, `select`, or a checkbox with `role="switch"`. There
are no component params: `type`, `name`, `id`, `placeholder`, `required`,
`disabled`, `rows`, `checked` and the rest are the element's own native
attributes, written inline (or via the matching Rails tag helper).

## Do and don't

- Do wire every `label` with `for:` to the field's `id` — clicking
  the label must focus the field.
- Do use the right `type:` on `input` (date, number, email) — it
  buys native keyboards, validation and pickers.
- Do put format examples in `placeholder` and rules in help text —
  never use the placeholder as the label (see *Content*).
- Don't pre-select a `select` without a reason; use `prompt:` and
  let the user choose. Defaults are answers — wrong defaults get
  submitted.
- Don't restyle a field with ad-hoc classes — apply the basecoat
  class so every field stays consistent.

## Accessibility and content

- All native elements: keyboard interaction, form submission and
  screen-reader semantics come from the platform.
- `required` renders the real attribute — the browser enforces it
  before the server does.
- The switch's `role="switch"` announces on/off rather than
  checked/unchecked; its label should name the thing switched
  ("Email notifications"), not an action.
